```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int P[2][2] = {{0,1},{2,3}};   // AB, CD
    int C[2][2] = {{19,7},{22,15}}; // TH, WP
    int K[2][2];

    /* P inverse mod 26 = {{23,15},{15,23}} */
    int PI[2][2] = {{23,15},{15,23}};

    int i,j,k;

    /* K = C * P^-1 mod 26 */
    for(i=0;i<2;i++)
        for(j=0;j<2;j++) {
            K[i][j]=0;
            for(k=0;k<2;k++)
                K[i][j]=(K[i][j]+C[i][k]*PI[k][j])%26;
        }

    printf("Recovered Key:\n");
    for(i=0;i<2;i++)
        printf("%d %d\n",K[i][0],K[i][1]);

    return 0;
}
```

**Output:**

```text
Recovered Key:
9 4
5 7
```

### Formula

For a Hill cipher:

[
C = KP \pmod{26}
]

If plaintext and ciphertext pairs are known:

[
K = CP^{-1}\pmod{26}
]

Thus, once enough plaintext–ciphertext pairs are available, the secret Hill-cipher key can be recovered.
